 4. A cruise awakening 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Image courtesy of Villa Vie Residences

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Villa Vie Residences' continuous world cruise has run out of gas, Pete writes .

 Zoom in: The Florida-based company — which offers "permanent residency" in rooms ranging from roughly $120,000 to $440,000, plus thousands more a month in fees — has ordered residents off its 640-foot cruise ship while at port in Cambodia.

 ⛽️ The reason: It can't afford to refuel. Catch up quick: The ship, the Villa Vie Odyssey, was two years into a planned three-and-a-half year long "continuous voyage around the world."

 It suspended operations yesterday as it "works to address extraordinary increases in fuel costs and evaluate restructuring and recapitalization alternatives." What they're saying: The Odyssey has been operating in South Asia since August, per its itinerary, and the company said fuel costs in the region "have risen sharply."

 Monthly fuel expenses, measured per equivalent cabin, have more than tripled from historic levels, it says. 🗣️ "Since the [Iran] conflict began in late February, we have taken significant steps to reduce fuel consumption, including slowing the itinerary and spending more time in port," the company said. "Those measures have not been enough to offset the extraordinary increase in fuel costs, and we have been unable to continue carrying the resulting operating losses." 💭 Pete's thought bubble: Homeownership is expensive enough without having to worry about a quarter-million gallons of fuel.

 
 
